WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.850
So it's 9 o'clock on the West Coast.

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:08.890
So good morning, everyone, and good afternoon
to those who are joining us from the East

00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:09.890
Coast.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:10.890
I'm Alex Bui.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.880
I'm the co-director of the BD2K Centers Coordination
Center.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.269
So we're wrapping up section two of our Data
Science Center, which to remind you has been

00:00:18.269 --> 00:00:20.619
an overview of data representation issues.

00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:25.239
We've covered a spectrum of different topics,
starting with databases and data warehousing

00:00:25.239 --> 00:00:29.859
challenges, through to issues related to data
wrangling and exploration.

00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:34.460
And today we're going to hear about natural
language processing, or NLP, from Dr. Noemie

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.860
Elhadad of Columbia University.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.770
And for any of you who have had to deal with
either unstructured clinical reports or other

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:46.330
types of data, such as biomedical literature,
you can already appreciate many of the issues

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:49.830
of trying to get a computer to understand
a textual document.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:54.920
NLP has been and continues to be an active
area of research, particularly in regards

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.260
to the biomedical space.

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.380
So it's my pleasure to introduce Dr. Elhadad.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:03.670
She is an associate professor of biomedical
informatics at Columbia.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:08.210
And as a data scientist, her efforts focus
on the development of techniques, smart clinicians,

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:13.789
patients, and health researchers in the information
workflows by automatically extracting and

00:01:13.789 --> 00:01:17.979
making accessible information from large clinical
datasets.

00:01:17.979 --> 00:01:21.850
Two particular areas of interest in her research
include computational approaches that infer

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:27.069
models of health phenomena and that account
for the specific biases seen in large health

00:01:27.069 --> 00:01:31.450
datasets and the translation of these models
into actionable knowledge and applications.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:34.569
Dr. Elhadah, we're delighted to have you with
us this morning.

00:01:34.569 --> 00:01:36.700
And so without further ado, let's get started.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.249
The floor is yours.

00:01:38.249 --> 00:01:39.249
Great.

00:01:39.249 --> 00:01:40.249
Thank you, Alex.

00:01:40.249 --> 00:01:41.249
Hi everyone.

00:01:41.249 --> 00:01:46.270
So I'm going to be talking to you about natural
language processing and NLP in health in particular.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:54.380
And the way I think I'm going to structure
this lecture is into four topics.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.609
And I'm going to spend most of my time on
actually the first two topics.

00:01:57.609 --> 00:02:02.849
The first one is to kind of give you an overview
of the applications of natural language processing

00:02:02.849 --> 00:02:05.020
in health and health care.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:12.760
It's a very active field of research for core
NLP methods, but for the sake of this audience

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:18.610
here, I think it's interesting to think about
all the different ways in which texts and

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:26.000
semi-structured texts can be leveraged for
advancing knowledge of biomedical sources

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.680
or for helping clinicians in their daily care
of patients.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:36.060
I am then going to be spending quite some
time on talking with you through some examples

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:42.000
of why computing language is difficult, and
I'll give you an example both in the general

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.000
and in the health domain, too.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.990
And then I'll move on to some approaches in
health natural language processing.

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:52.200
There's been a shift in
computing approaches, and so it's interesting

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.280
to kind of give an overview of those.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:02.410
And I'll conclude with the current developments
in both domain-generic NLP and health NLP

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:03.510
research.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:10.820
So let me just start by saying something that
might be obvious to you by now as human beings,

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.510
which is that language is primitive.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:21.570
As women, when we talk to each other, we use
language in written and not written form,

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:26.820
and the big revolution for us in the computing
world is that there is more and more of such

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.600
instances of written text available to us.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:37.510
And so this is an example of a J-identified
clinical note.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:45.040
And the things to notice here which are going
to become a themes is that it's English, but

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:46.850
it's not your typical English, right?

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:54.170
So like there's a lot of abbreviations like
HPI, which means history of present illness,

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:59.700
and so if I read, if I were to read this first
sentence, I would say 77-year-old male, with

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:14.080
history of hypertension, coronary artery disease,
status post coronary artery bypass left, 1988,

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:17.100
meaning that happened in 1988.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:21.620
And so you can already kind of think about
the challenges that we have here, right?

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:28.460
We are trying to maybe understand or extract
some information from these type of texts,

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:34.540
and we have to somehow be able to encode or
to reason about the fact that this note is

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:40.460
about a male patient of that particular age,
at the time the note was written, and some

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:46.380
procedures were given to the patient, like
the CABG, and we actually didn't have timing

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:52.620
for these procedures, but there are also information
about medications, symptoms, disorders, et

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.450
cetera, et cetera.

00:04:55.450 --> 00:05:03.020
Another really important source of pretext
for people who do NLP is the literature, the

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.020
scientific literature.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:12.750
And here maybe the language is more structured,
but it's still a very specific genre of text.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.510
And there are some things that we can leverage.

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:21.030
We know that scientific articles have some
structure to them, their backgrounds, methods,

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:23.420
results, conclusions, et cetera.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:31.150
But they also have slightly complex sentences
from a syntactic standpoint.

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:37.590
And so if we want to be able to understand
what type of information is being conveyed

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:44.840
in these articles and do so at scale across
many articles, then we need to probably be

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:49.440
able to parse these sentences accurately.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:59.940
Health news are becoming an important source
of knowledge and trends in health and other

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:01.960
domains as well.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:08.710
I'm sure you know outside of health, there's
all these interesting new things happening

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:10.210
with fake news, for example.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:12.280
So how do we detect that something is a fake
news?

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:18.140
How do we detect that vitamin B12 is indeed
correlated with higher cancer risk?

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:24.730
You could think of the health news as a way
to disseminate information that comes with

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:27.570
science, but with a very biased slant.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:35.080
And interestingly, from an NLP standpoint,
we see yet a different style of language.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:40.550
And finally another type of text that is of
interest to natural language processing people

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:47.770
is all the information that patients and health
consumers are exchanging on the social web.

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:52.450
So this is an example from a publicly available
breast cancer community.

00:06:52.450 --> 00:07:01.919
And I like this example because it kind of
describes to us in a very clear fashion what

00:07:01.919 --> 00:07:08.770
some of our issues are going to be if we want
to, if we want to parse this type of text.

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:13.510
And so this sounds like a very exciting--
this is a thread.

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:17.880
ChainsawZ is the first person writing, and
she's very happy.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:23.030
She said about someone else that she's glad
you found reggie but I hope you don't consume

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:26.720
for the twoto-one NED or NED.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:28.500
And it sounds like it's not about cancer.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:34.070
It actually is about breast cancer, but what
happens here is a common phenomenon in online

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:38.860
communities, where the community is coming
up with their own abbreviations and nearly

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:41.430
their own register of language.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:48.699
And so in fact "reggie" is regression of tumor,
and NED or NED is "no evidence of disease."

00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:55.180
And so this is referring to the fact that
someone has regression of her tumor and everyone

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:56.180
is very happy.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:08:02.060
There's a lot of positive sentiment, which
can be easily identified automatically.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:08.580
But we also would love to be able to automatically
identify that this person we're talking about

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:13.630
had a regression in her tumor.

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:21.140
So what are some of the applications that
have been heavily using NLP?

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:30.260
One that is a classic application and a very
robust one by no and still some time for interesting

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:35.830
reason is becoming yet again a new question
is automated coding of clinical notes.

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:43.610
So the task here is I have a clinical note,
typically a discharge summary.

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:52.339
And on the other hand, I have a very large
taxonomy of diagnosis codes, or ICD-9 codes,

00:08:52.339 --> 00:08:59.840
and I would like to be able to automatically
say what are the most, say, eight or 10 or

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:05.140
15 likely codes that can help me bill for
a visit or that can help me represent what

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:07.510
this note is about.

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:12.880
So from a computing standpoint, the difficulty
here is twofold.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:19.250
On one hand, we have free text, and the second
difficulty is that it's not a simple text

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:25.740
categorization task because it's multilevel
classification.

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:28.990
That universe of labels is really, really
wide.

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:32.680
So ICD-9 codes, there's about 15,000 of them.

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:38.130
And I'm sure you guys know, we actually in
the United States have moved to ICD-10 coding,

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:41.750
where now we're talking about 65,000-plus
codes.

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:48.370
So again from a computing standpoint, how
do we train algorithms to recognize what are

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:55.190
the most likely codes is an interesting question.

00:09:55.190 --> 00:10:01.720
In a very classical question of NLP, where
NLP has been used again in the clinical world,

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:04.180
is one of cohort detection.

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:10.029
And I know you have had a lecture on information
retrievals, so I'm not going to go too much

00:10:10.029 --> 00:10:11.730
into it, but it's an important one.

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:19.170
By the way, I gave examples of articles that
I think represent very well what the NLP challenges

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:21.310
are and what the tasks or methods are.

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:25.030
And so you're welcome to go and look at them,
but they're just simple examples.

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:30.279
There's actually quite a lot of research on
each of those areas.

00:10:30.279 --> 00:10:36.290
And so here again the interesting fact is
that if we want to select a cohort, say from

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:37.320
the electronic health records.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:43.620
So if we want to identify in the electronic
health record, who are the patients who have

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:44.620
a particular disease?

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:48.190
In this example, we're talking about peripheral
arterial disease.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:53.770
Then we know we could use some of the metadata
that's available to us, but we also would

00:10:53.770 --> 00:10:58.470
probably want to use or leverage the clinical
notes.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:03.750
And as a matter of fact, it turns out that
when it comes to identifying cohorts, the

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:07.980
NLP's often quite critical to identifying
the right population.

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:08.980
Clinical decision support is a traditional
example of clinical informatics, where again

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:09.980
NLP would be useful.

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:12.610
The idea
Clinical decision support is a traditional

00:11:12.610 --> 00:11:19.649
example of clinical informatics, where again
NLP would be useful.

00:11:19.649 --> 00:11:25.100
The idea here is to leverage information from
clinical notes within the logic of clinical

00:11:25.100 --> 00:11:26.100
decisions support.

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:32.589
So if we want to have an alert that looks
at potentially dangerous drug-drug interactions

00:11:32.589 --> 00:11:38.370
at the time of prescription, in the electronic
health record, or if we want to make sure

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:43.970
that we're not prescribing the drug when a
patient has a specific allergy, this type

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:51.310
of clinical decision support system will go
and look at the structured information or

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:55.910
does it make that data available in the electronic
health record?

00:11:55.910 --> 00:12:02.100
But again, there is no guarantee that all
the information about the patient is included

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:05.020
in the structured part of the EHR.

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:14.100
And in fact, very often, old allergies or
old things kind of fall through the cracks,

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.800
and are documented in the notes, but not in
the structured part.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:20.760
And so NLP here, again, is extremely helpful.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:24.410
Another type of application is one of data
exploration.

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:30.530
And this comes from the fact that there is
so much text available to clinicians, to house

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:37.930
practitioners, to researchers, that we need
to help and support them in just making sense

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:39.500
of all the data that's available.

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:46.620
So this is an example of a patient's record
summarization system that we have at Columbia

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:47.620
University.

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:51.709
And it's actually deployed and used by physicians.

00:12:51.709 --> 00:12:57.180
And the idea here is that we're taking all
of the notes longitudinally.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:03.100
We have 20-plus years worth of data for each
of our patients.

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:10.370
And we can, through natural language processing,
identify what are the primary problems for

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:19.810
that patient, and on top of it, have an interactive
visualization that allows the clinicians and

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:28.029
EHR workers to explore and go through the
timeline of a patient.

00:13:28.029 --> 00:13:33.740
This is another example of how NLP can be
used for data exploration.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:36.310
This is in a slightly different genre of text.

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:40.200
We're looking at autism spectrum disorder
communities.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:48.670
So these are parents of children, typically,
who are on the ASD spectrum.

00:13:48.670 --> 00:13:50.790
And the question, again, is there is a lot
of discussions.

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:53.790
How do I make sense of all of these discussions?

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:55.890
Why would I want to do this?

00:13:55.890 --> 00:14:02.050
Maybe I'm a public health practitioner, and
I want to look into health communication issues.

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:08.040
Or maybe I want to learn about how parents
are viewing certain treatments.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:15.589
Or what is the awareness about particular
disorders or symptoms for patients?

00:14:15.589 --> 00:14:23.810
And so here what we've done is, we've created
a set of named entity recognition system that

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:30.110
can identify terms in posts, and so on the
top right here you see an example a snippet

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:31.540
from a post.

00:14:31.540 --> 00:14:38.260
And we then did so for all the posts in the
community, and built, again, a visualization

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:44.839
to help with exploration on top that kind
of builds a network of significantly occurring

00:14:44.839 --> 00:14:48.630
pairs of terms.

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:53.200
And so in this case, we were in, again, an
autism community.

00:14:53.200 --> 00:15:00.760
And [INAUDIBLE] is very specific to the language
and the register and the domain of autism.

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:08.209
But it's interesting to see in which context,
basically, that type of term is being talked

00:15:08.209 --> 00:15:09.540
about.

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:16.329
This is another example of data exploration
from text that has been extremely successful

00:15:16.329 --> 00:15:18.709
in many different areas, actually.

00:15:18.709 --> 00:15:23.090
And this is one of topic modeling.

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:30.420
And so this is taken from a paper that looked
at all of the publications from Science journal,

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:38.089
and is trying to make sense, in a very unsupervised
fashion, about all the different topics or

00:15:38.089 --> 00:15:48.380
themes that have been talked about in the
Science journal over 100 plus years.

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:50.029
So what is the challenge here?

00:15:50.029 --> 00:15:53.410
The challenge is that we do not know in advance.

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:54.790
We're completely unsupervised.

00:15:54.790 --> 00:15:56.440
We do not know in advance.

00:15:56.440 --> 00:16:02.589
We're not making any hypotheses about what
type of themes are being discussed in these

00:16:02.589 --> 00:16:03.639
articles.

00:16:03.639 --> 00:16:08.829
And we want a method that can allow us to
identify what are the primary themes.

00:16:08.829 --> 00:16:13.950
And moreover, in the case of correlated topic
models like in this example, we want to know

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:17.279
how these themes relate to each other.

00:16:17.279 --> 00:16:25.290
And so we can build this type, again, of visualizations
that tells us that RNA, DNA, and that type

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:33.430
of cluster of words here correspond to a significant
topic or a cluster.

00:16:33.430 --> 00:16:40.110
And moreover, it is quite relevant related
to-- meaning it occurs or it correlates often--

00:16:40.110 --> 00:16:47.170
with a topic about sequencing and genetic
sequencing, and another one about proteins,

00:16:47.170 --> 00:16:49.269
for example.

00:16:49.269 --> 00:16:56.760
So the value here is that when you have a
very, very large corpus given to you, for

00:16:56.760 --> 00:17:03.839
example, all of the articles published in
a journal, and you're trying to extract, to

00:17:03.839 --> 00:17:08.770
understand what are the primary topics discussed
in that journal, like in this example, or

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:14.850
if you have a patient cohort with lots of
anatomic health-related notes related to them,

00:17:14.850 --> 00:17:22.829
or if you have access to an online house community,
for example, prior to detecting a particular

00:17:22.829 --> 00:17:27.600
phenomenon, maybe you want to just explore
and understand what is being talked about.

00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:35.110
And so a lot of NLP methods rely on this type
of topic modeling for data exploration.

00:17:35.110 --> 00:17:39.880
So another thing that you can do with this
type of topic modeling is you can then look

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:41.730
at how they change through time.

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:47.830
So again, this is from the Science journal
article, where you can look at-- these are

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:50.030
examples of two different topics.

00:17:50.030 --> 00:17:56.620
And you can look at, through time, from the
earliest years of publication of the Science

00:17:56.620 --> 00:18:04.490
journal in 1880 up to 2000 what, for example,
the mention of the term "oxygen," and what

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:09.380
was basically the interest, maybe, of oxygen
in the journal.

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:11.990
So keep in mind, we're not extracting information
here.

00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:18.280
We're just identifying kind of salient terms
that are representative of the corpus here.

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:26.350
And again, it should not be interpreted as
a literal representation of the corpus, but

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:29.030
rather as a tool for data exploration.

00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:37.540
And a more and more prevalent type of application
has to come with surveillance from publicly

00:18:37.540 --> 00:18:39.350
available data sets.

00:18:39.350 --> 00:18:42.860
This is a very active field of research.

00:18:42.860 --> 00:18:49.900
I'm putting here in just two examples of papers,
but there are many, many more, where the authors

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:57.040
are anything at public social media sources,
so things like Twitter or Yelp, and trying

00:18:57.040 --> 00:19:01.470
to identify very specific occurrences of a
condition.

00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:08.110
In this case, the authors and the Department
of Health and [INAUDIBLE] are interested in

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:14.271
identifying who has had food-borne illness
after eating in a restaurant, so that they

00:19:14.271 --> 00:19:21.450
can 
go and investigate what's going on with that

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:22.450
restaurant.

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:26.460
So what are the challenges again here, from
an NLP standpoint?

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:30.200
Well, on the top left, you see a Yelp review.

00:19:30.200 --> 00:19:34.790
And we want to, knowing that we're looking
at food-borne illness, we want to be able

00:19:34.790 --> 00:19:40.880
to go and extract that someone was sick, but
not only just generally sick, but sick from

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:41.880
eating.

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:48.690
And not only that, the symptoms of foodborne
illness kind of happened after enough time

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:56.330
that it would kind of support the idea that
food-borne illness came from that restaurant.

00:19:56.330 --> 00:19:59.950
So it's a complex information extraction test.

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:03.600
There is a bit of reasoning involved in here.

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:10.200
Another challenge is that, especially if we're
talking about Twitter, not all of the information

00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:13.740
is going to be nicely contained into a single
text.

00:20:13.740 --> 00:20:19.580
And there is going to be, maybe, conversations,
and all sorts of interesting linguistic phenomena,

00:20:19.580 --> 00:20:25.500
such as coreferences that are going to be
needed to be taken care of to identify, for

00:20:25.500 --> 00:20:31.270
example, like in this example on the bottom
right here, that this person indeed got sick,

00:20:31.270 --> 00:20:38.570
and that that was a particular location where
the restaurant was the potential culprit for

00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:43.170
the food-borne illness.

00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:47.419
Researchers have been thinking about this
idea of info surveillance quite a lot.

00:20:47.419 --> 00:20:51.210
This is HealthMap, which I encourage you to
go and visit.

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:53.840
This is work from Harvard and John Brownstein.

00:20:53.840 --> 00:20:58.090
And if you Google HealthMap, you'll find it
right away.

00:20:58.090 --> 00:21:04.090
And it's a very nice interactive visualization
of all the potential diseases that are being

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:09.020
talked about, typically on the news.

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:13.150
But they are incorporating other data sources
as well in here.

00:21:13.150 --> 00:21:16.470
And so again, the same challenges come again.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:21.190
How do we identify that a particular disease
is being talked about?

00:21:21.190 --> 00:21:31.789
And moreover, in this case, what is the location
of where the patients are being notified about?

00:21:31.789 --> 00:21:36.760
So let me switch gears and continue with my
summary of applications.

00:21:36.760 --> 00:21:40.020
This is going back to the clinical domain.

00:21:40.020 --> 00:21:42.620
We're interested in high throughput phenotyping.

00:21:42.620 --> 00:21:45.770
Why do we want to do high throughput phenotyping?

00:21:45.770 --> 00:21:51.560
We want to maybe be able to identify all conditions
that are being talked about across a large

00:21:51.560 --> 00:21:52.780
cohort of patients.

00:21:52.780 --> 00:22:01.070
Or maybe we want to do that [INAUDIBLE] identification,
but instead of finding methods to do so very

00:22:01.070 --> 00:22:05.549
well for one disease at a time, maybe we want
to do it for all diseases at once.

00:22:05.549 --> 00:22:09.950
And the reason I'm bringing up this example
is mostly because the methods are very similar

00:22:09.950 --> 00:22:12.299
to the one of topic
modeling.

00:22:12.299 --> 00:22:19.470
And they're interesting, because they're taking
into account not only the text, but also the

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:25.321
other data structure information that we have
in the electronic health records, such as

00:22:25.321 --> 00:22:27.820
laboratory tests, medications, and diagnosis
notes.

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:33.559
And so we can build NLP-based methods that
kind of incorporate this additional information,

00:22:33.559 --> 00:22:42.039
and bring better cues, basically, to identify
what are the observations that enable us to

00:22:42.039 --> 00:22:50.090
decide that there is probably diabetes mellitus
Type 2 for a particular patient, for example.

00:22:50.090 --> 00:22:53.040
And so in this case, it's very much like topic
modeling.

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:57.020
It is an unsupervised method.

00:22:57.020 --> 00:23:03.320
And we can, moreover, kind of have a description
of those typical conditions or phenotypes

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:04.320
we are looking for.

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:05.830
So for example here, we have lupus.

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:12.880
And we're finding, again, in a very unsupervised
fashion, that anti-malarial are a medication

00:23:12.880 --> 00:23:15.509
class that actually makes sense for lupus.

00:23:15.509 --> 00:23:20.410
And for the non-clinicians out there, actually,
that's correct.

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:24.150
Anti-malarial are a way of treating lupus.

00:23:24.150 --> 00:23:30.200
Another type of application which is really,
really big in health care, but is becoming

00:23:30.200 --> 00:23:40.809
really big also on social media type of situations
is predictive analytics.

00:23:40.809 --> 00:23:47.590
So the question here is, can we use text--
either text which involves a patient or in

00:23:47.590 --> 00:23:55.350
this case, in this example that I'm giving,
text that are written by individuals on reddit.

00:23:55.350 --> 00:24:02.440
And can I predict whether something is likely
to happen, or they're likely to behave in

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:03.440
a certain fashion.

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:07.940
So in this example, we are interested in mental
health.

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:15.720
And the authors are looking at subreddit that
have people talking about their depression.

00:24:15.720 --> 00:24:22.870
And then they want to know within six months,
are they actually now moving on to another

00:24:22.870 --> 00:24:26.090
type of subreddit which is more about suicide
watch.

00:24:26.090 --> 00:24:31.159
And so the question here is, can I predict
that someone is going to start ideating about

00:24:31.159 --> 00:24:33.169
suicide?

00:24:33.169 --> 00:24:39.309
And it turns out that there are very specific
linguistic indicators that can help us predict

00:24:39.309 --> 00:24:45.490
who is going to be likely to move on from
the mental health type of subreddit to the

00:24:45.490 --> 00:24:47.540
suicide watch type of subreddit.

00:24:47.540 --> 00:24:53.280
You can imagine, there's a lot of interesting
applications that can come out of this work.

00:24:53.280 --> 00:25:01.280
But what's nice is that there's these cues
that come from the syntax, or from the type

00:25:01.280 --> 00:25:07.750
of words that are being used by people, where
we can actually predict what's going to happen

00:25:07.750 --> 00:25:10.630
in their behavior in an online community.

00:25:10.630 --> 00:25:19.240
Similarly, in the clinical world, we can,
again, try to predict adverse outcomes.

00:25:19.240 --> 00:25:26.430
So here, this is an example of a predictive
model that looks at progression of chronic

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:28.150
kidney disease.

00:25:28.150 --> 00:25:32.600
And what's interesting here is that, because
we're in the clinical world, we want to be

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:38.400
able to predict, but we also want to be able
to interpret some of our learned rules.

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:47.039
And so the type of methods that are used here
are, again, unsupervised, and are basically

00:25:47.039 --> 00:25:52.100
learning topics together with a kind of a
temporal model, which is a common filter in

00:25:52.100 --> 00:25:53.150
this case.

00:25:53.150 --> 00:25:59.290
But primarily for NLP purposes, the
point is that we can learn topics of words

00:25:59.290 --> 00:26:06.490
that occur in clinical notes that are going
to be more or less predictive or increasing

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:13.350
risks for progression-- those are the orange/red
topics in here-- and the ones that are going

00:26:13.350 --> 00:26:17.260
to have a productive effect on patients.

00:26:17.260 --> 00:26:23.039
And so that's, again, useful, because we can
go further, generate hypotheses, and work

00:26:23.039 --> 00:26:26.090
further with clinicians on these questions.

00:26:26.090 --> 00:26:29.530
I spent a lot of time on those applications.

00:26:29.530 --> 00:26:30.760
But I think it's useful.

00:26:30.760 --> 00:26:33.419
And again, there's many, many others.

00:26:33.419 --> 00:26:38.800
It's just useful, hopefully, for you-- and
it's definitely helpful for me in my research--

00:26:38.800 --> 00:26:44.919
to think very widely about all the different
types of texts as data or seasoned knowledge

00:26:44.919 --> 00:26:48.470
versus the various types of tasks we want
to to.

00:26:48.470 --> 00:26:55.260
But I want to now focus on, well, OK, so we
know that there are lots of applications to

00:26:55.260 --> 00:27:00.640
natural language processing, but why is it
so hard?

00:27:00.640 --> 00:27:08.780
And more specifically, why can't I just use
regular expressions?

00:27:08.780 --> 00:27:17.520
Regular expressions are just like models of
what I expect a set of string or characters

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:21.890
is going to look like, and it's going to help
me search for a particular type of information

00:27:21.890 --> 00:27:24.270
on a large collection of text, for example.

00:27:24.270 --> 00:27:28.140
Why do I need more complex models of language?

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:32.491
Well, the first problem is that language is
ambiguous.

00:27:32.491 --> 00:27:36.470
And it's ambiguous at all linguistic levels.

00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:42.740
And for those interested in what I mean by
linguistic levels, morphology, lexical levels

00:27:42.740 --> 00:27:48.760
in texts, discourse and pragmatics are all
interesting levels to think about.

00:27:48.760 --> 00:27:51.400
And here are some of the examples that are
the classic examples.

00:27:51.400 --> 00:27:57.190
These examples are taken actually taken from
the excellent, excellent course from Chris

00:27:57.190 --> 00:28:01.039
Manning at Stanford on Natural Language Processing.

00:28:01.039 --> 00:28:04.990
And so here is one where there's definitely
some syntax you can [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:04.990 --> 00:28:10.890
Boy paralyzed after tumor fights back to gain
black belt.

00:28:10.890 --> 00:28:16.310
Same for this other headline in the news,
"San Jose Cops Kill Man with a Knife."

00:28:16.310 --> 00:28:20.650
So where is the ambiguity here?

00:28:20.650 --> 00:28:24.940
Well, are we saying who had the knife, basically?

00:28:24.940 --> 00:28:28.580
Are we saying that the cops had a knife, and
that's how they killed the man?

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:32.290
Are we saying that the cops killed a man who
had a knife?

00:28:32.290 --> 00:28:36.150
And so that type of ambiguity is called PP
attachment ambiguity.

00:28:36.150 --> 00:28:41.820
We don't know if we were to build a syntactic
parse for that sentence, we don't know where

00:28:41.820 --> 00:28:44.390
we should attach that phrase "with knife."

00:28:44.390 --> 00:28:48.060
Should we attach it to the noun phrase, "man?"

00:28:48.060 --> 00:28:51.680
or should we attach it to the noun phrase,
"San Jose cops?"

00:28:51.680 --> 00:28:53.350
It's actually ambiguous.

00:28:53.350 --> 00:28:55.860
We do not know.

00:28:55.860 --> 00:29:03.800
We do know, maybe, as humans when we read
it, because maybe there is some more likely

00:29:03.800 --> 00:29:04.880
scenario.

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:05.880
But it's still ambiguous.

00:29:05.880 --> 00:29:10.130
There could be a scenario where the cops are
killing the man with a knife.

00:29:10.130 --> 00:29:12.059
Sorry, that was ambiguous, too.

00:29:12.059 --> 00:29:18.490
They are killing a man, and they're using
a knife to kill him.

00:29:18.490 --> 00:29:25.860
Some of our more clinical examples, where
ambiguity creeps in are very lexical for us

00:29:25.860 --> 00:29:27.930
in clinical texts.

00:29:27.930 --> 00:29:32.030
This is an
actual example from a social history of a

00:29:32.030 --> 00:29:36.460
patient, and this patient's history-- CA in
mother, breast CA.

00:29:36.460 --> 00:29:43.460
And so we have here a term, which is CA, which
happens to be an abbreviation.

00:29:43.460 --> 00:29:48.120
And those type of abbreviations are usually
fairly ambiguous.

00:29:48.120 --> 00:29:49.539
In the first occurrence of CA, we mean California.

00:29:49.539 --> 00:29:54.260
In the second one, we mean cancer, and in
particular, breast cancer.

00:29:54.260 --> 00:30:03.440
So how do we build a language model that can
help us disambiguate between these cases?

00:30:03.440 --> 00:30:11.260
Moreover there is other senses to this type
of text, to this type of terms.

00:30:11.260 --> 00:30:13.780
For example, CA also means calcium.

00:30:13.780 --> 00:30:19.850
So this lexical ambiguity is quite difficult.

00:30:19.850 --> 00:30:22.700
The other challenge with language is the one
of variability.

00:30:22.700 --> 00:30:26.580
And again, it happens at all linguistic levels.

00:30:26.580 --> 00:30:31.539
Another way to think of it is that there's
a high paraphrasing power in language.

00:30:31.539 --> 00:30:37.779
And it's a good thing, actually, because as
humans, we want to have ability to create

00:30:37.779 --> 00:30:41.950
new ways of saying new things, but also old
things.

00:30:41.950 --> 00:30:48.919
There usually are some reasons to why you
would say a sentence one way or another.

00:30:48.919 --> 00:30:56.570
But for the sake of computing, we want to
be able to identify that maybe different forms

00:30:56.570 --> 00:31:02.030
of a term actually refer to the same concept,
like on the example on the left.

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:10.520
Diabetes mellitus 2, diabetes, T2 DM-- all
of these three and many others-- how can I

00:31:10.520 --> 00:31:16.120
build a model that recognizes that these guys
are all talking about type 2 diabetes?

00:31:16.120 --> 00:31:20.929
And note that I put diabetes here as an example.

00:31:20.929 --> 00:31:21.929
And that's actually ambiguous.

00:31:21.929 --> 00:31:24.640
Are we talking about type 1 or type 2 diabetes?

00:31:24.640 --> 00:31:31.370
And so maybe there's some additional reasoning
that would tell me that in my corpus, really

00:31:31.370 --> 00:31:34.020
when I see diabetes, I mean type 2 diabetes.

00:31:34.020 --> 00:31:41.539
And I'm able to map or normalize all of these
forms to a single concept.

00:31:41.539 --> 00:31:45.260
And so that's an example of variability from
the lexical standpoint.

00:31:45.260 --> 00:31:50.320
But there is, obviously, a lot of variability
in syntactically.

00:31:50.320 --> 00:31:51.590
John loves Mary.

00:31:51.590 --> 00:31:53.399
Mary is loved by John.

00:31:53.399 --> 00:31:55.429
The man who is named John loves Mary.

00:31:55.429 --> 00:31:57.669
All these things mean the same thing.

00:31:57.669 --> 00:32:04.020
They're paraphrases of each other, and are,
nevertheless, conveyed with different syntactic

00:32:04.020 --> 00:32:06.670
structures.

00:32:06.670 --> 00:32:12.539
It's interesting to think further about the
lexical ambiguity, and really go back to that

00:32:12.539 --> 00:32:15.510
question about diabetes.

00:32:15.510 --> 00:32:17.980
Do we mean diabetes type 2 in general?

00:32:17.980 --> 00:32:21.390
So the context is really helpful in these
things.

00:32:21.390 --> 00:32:27.649
And as a matter of fact, a lot of the computational
methods are incorporating context to determine

00:32:27.649 --> 00:32:29.420
this type of language models.

00:32:29.420 --> 00:32:34.529
And so I thought I would show you a very simple
visualization of what we mean by context.

00:32:34.529 --> 00:32:38.950
So here we have two online health communities.

00:32:38.950 --> 00:32:45.669
One is the same Autism Spectrum Disorder discussion
forum that I mentioned earlier.

00:32:45.669 --> 00:32:49.230
And the other one is a general parenting discussion
forum.

00:32:49.230 --> 00:32:56.570
And again, we built a representation of all
the terms that are being mentioned

00:32:56.570 --> 00:32:58.670
in those forums.

00:32:58.670 --> 00:33:06.020
And we do some normalization, as much as we
can, but we also do then try to look for co-occurrence

00:33:06.020 --> 00:33:07.440
patterns.

00:33:07.440 --> 00:33:13.539
And so two terms are going to happen, in this
network, an edge between them if they co-occur

00:33:13.539 --> 00:33:17.809
together more often than they do not.

00:33:17.809 --> 00:33:21.049
And so why am I putting these two networks
here?

00:33:21.049 --> 00:33:27.250
Those are networks centered, actually, on
a single node-- on the node "drug" here.

00:33:27.250 --> 00:33:34.240
And similarly here on the ASD on your left,
about the node "drug" but as it is mentioned

00:33:34.240 --> 00:33:36.810
in the autism communities.

00:33:36.810 --> 00:33:37.930
And so what do we see?

00:33:37.930 --> 00:33:40.980
Well, it's two very, very different networks.

00:33:40.980 --> 00:33:44.940
And clearly, you can say a lot about, even
though they are exactly the same terms, that

00:33:44.940 --> 00:33:49.070
they mean completely different things in different
contexts.

00:33:49.070 --> 00:33:55.960
And so in the general parenting forum, "drug"
is typically meant as an illicit or illegal

00:33:55.960 --> 00:34:02.149
drug, or a problematic drug like alcohol,
marijuana, substance abuse.

00:34:02.149 --> 00:34:09.099
Pharmaceutical company's more about the pharma
type of drug and illicit.

00:34:09.099 --> 00:34:14.219
Those are the terms that are found as being
statistically important related to "drug."

00:34:14.219 --> 00:34:19.309
In the autism world, clearly, they mean different
types of drugs.

00:34:19.309 --> 00:34:24.429
And moreover, all of these drugs are very
representative of the type of treatments that

00:34:24.429 --> 00:34:30.299
children on the spectrum are using.

00:34:30.299 --> 00:34:37.999
So again, from an NLP standpoint, the interest
here is that, even though there are terms

00:34:37.999 --> 00:34:44.999
that have exactly the same lexical information,
they actually mean different things in different

00:34:44.999 --> 00:34:45.999
contexts.

00:34:45.999 --> 00:34:49.870
And so a lot of the methods that are being
developed nowadays have to do with language

00:34:49.870 --> 00:34:58.920
modeling, with trying to represent or encode
what is the meaning of the particular word

00:34:58.920 --> 00:35:04.660
through what type of words they occur or co-occur
with.

00:35:04.660 --> 00:35:08.390
This is a similar example again.

00:35:08.390 --> 00:35:12.829
Same word on both sides means very, very different
things in two types of context.

00:35:12.829 --> 00:35:18.730
In general parenting, when parents talk about
language, they mean foul language or maybe

00:35:18.730 --> 00:35:19.900
bilingual language.

00:35:19.900 --> 00:35:26.979
Whereas in the autism community, language
is all about all of the potential symptoms

00:35:26.979 --> 00:35:37.910
related to autism, as well as potential therapies
that have to do with helping with language

00:35:37.910 --> 00:35:39.209
difficulties.

00:35:39.209 --> 00:35:48.619
The last challenge that I will focus on through
very simple examples is the fact that language

00:35:48.619 --> 00:35:50.319
is vague.

00:35:50.319 --> 00:35:54.569
And another way to think about it is that
maybe we need to reason about language.

00:35:54.569 --> 00:36:03.279
And like for the ambiguity and the variability
challenges, it's present at all lexical levels.

00:36:03.279 --> 00:36:07.089
So there's three types of example that I want
to focus on.

00:36:07.089 --> 00:36:13.109
The first one is one that we see quite a lot
in the clinical world.

00:36:13.109 --> 00:36:18.470
This is two sentences-- MI three weeks ago,
MI three days ago.

00:36:18.470 --> 00:36:19.930
So what's the question here?

00:36:19.930 --> 00:36:25.319
First of all, we need to recognize that MI
means of myocardial infarct in this case.

00:36:25.319 --> 00:36:26.319
That's fine.

00:36:26.319 --> 00:36:29.769
That's a lexical ambiguity or paraphrasing
[INAUDIBLE] question.

00:36:29.769 --> 00:36:36.089
But here, I want to focus on that temporal
expression here, which is three weeks ago.

00:36:36.089 --> 00:36:38.520
And here it says three days
ago.

00:36:38.520 --> 00:36:44.349
If we want to actually reason over this information,
and if we want to understand or represent

00:36:44.349 --> 00:36:51.799
the meaning of those occurrences, we need
to understand that first of all, this is a

00:36:51.799 --> 00:36:52.799
temporal expression.

00:36:52.799 --> 00:36:58.829
And moreover, it is about that clinical event
which was a myocardial infarct.

00:36:58.829 --> 00:37:03.049
The last thing you'd want to do, then, is
to say, well, OK, so if we were to establish

00:37:03.049 --> 00:37:11.219
a timeline of events of the patient, then
I would use a time at which the note was written,

00:37:11.219 --> 00:37:17.670
and I would go three weeks in the past, and
put the myocardial infarct at that time.

00:37:17.670 --> 00:37:20.440
That would be a sensible thing to do automatically.

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:25.809
It turns out that in the clinical world, that's
not always correct.

00:37:25.809 --> 00:37:29.640
That's not always the right thing to do.

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:33.430
I mean, it is the right thing to do, but it's
more complex than that.

00:37:33.430 --> 00:37:34.499
And so what's going on?

00:37:34.499 --> 00:37:41.239
Well, we can actually find that when someone
says, "MI three days ago," in fact, the person

00:37:41.239 --> 00:37:42.359
said three days ago.

00:37:42.359 --> 00:37:44.920
They really meant that it happened three days
ago.

00:37:44.920 --> 00:37:49.970
But when a clinician says three weeks ago,
it could be three weeks ago-- 21 days ago.

00:37:49.970 --> 00:37:54.869
But it could be 20 days ago, or 19 days ago,
or something like that.

00:37:54.869 --> 00:37:56.950
And it sounds like it's not an important problem.

00:37:56.950 --> 00:38:00.500
It actually is quite an important problem
for two reasons.

00:38:00.500 --> 00:38:05.819
One is that it's not always the case that
this type of temporal information is available

00:38:05.819 --> 00:38:08.059
in the structured part of the electronic health
record.

00:38:08.059 --> 00:38:12.960
We really want to rely on the notes, because
the notes are often talking about events that

00:38:12.960 --> 00:38:17.789
did not happen in the current institution,
or for various reasons.

00:38:17.789 --> 00:38:28.940
The second one is, if we want to study temporal
or time-related phenomena like adverse drug

00:38:28.940 --> 00:38:36.329
reactions or prediction of particular events,
we really want to have the timeline down correctly.

00:38:36.329 --> 00:38:44.089
And so how do we know that when someone says
"three weeks ago," that really meant 21 days

00:38:44.089 --> 00:38:49.359
plus or minus four days, whereas when someone
says "three days ago," that really means three

00:38:49.359 --> 00:38:51.420
days ago?

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:52.470
We need some reasoning here.

00:38:52.470 --> 00:38:59.380
Or at least we need a way to encode this type
of uncertainty about the language.

00:38:59.380 --> 00:39:04.119
Speaking of uncertainty, the next example
is interesting as well.

00:39:04.119 --> 00:39:11.280
And that's something that happens, actually,
very often in the clinical and in the health

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:12.280
domain.

00:39:12.280 --> 00:39:18.859
So when I say, "Paul thinks that John loves
Mary," there are a few things that are happening

00:39:18.859 --> 00:39:19.900
from a linguistic standpoint.

00:39:19.900 --> 00:39:24.709
First is that there is the fact that says
that John loves Mary.

00:39:24.709 --> 00:39:28.520
And now, what can we say about that fact?

00:39:28.520 --> 00:39:29.520
Is it true?

00:39:29.520 --> 00:39:30.520
Or is it untrue?

00:39:30.520 --> 00:39:33.279
What do we know exactly?

00:39:33.279 --> 00:39:36.749
We actually do not know whether that fact
is true.

00:39:36.749 --> 00:39:41.749
But we know that a third person thinks that
that fact is true.

00:39:41.749 --> 00:39:49.140
Similarly, this type of pragmatic things creep
up even in clinical NLP.

00:39:49.140 --> 00:39:57.380
When someone says, "Patient denies smoking,"
that's typically pharma's "Patient does not

00:39:57.380 --> 00:39:58.380
smoke."

00:39:58.380 --> 00:40:02.519
But really, when we think about it, it really
means, "I do not know whether the patient

00:40:02.519 --> 00:40:06.779
smokes or not, but I'm going to encode it,
and I'm going to represent it by saying the

00:40:06.779 --> 00:40:09.039
patient denies it."

00:40:09.039 --> 00:40:18.290
It's something to think about when we do this
type of information extraction and reasoning.

00:40:18.290 --> 00:40:21.809
In the health domain, that comes quite often.

00:40:21.809 --> 00:40:27.959
You can imagine if a patient says, write a
question online or searches for some symptoms

00:40:27.959 --> 00:40:33.190
somewhere, or writes to their peers, and they
often would say, "I think this is happening."

00:40:33.190 --> 00:40:38.109
And there's a lot of uncertainty about all
of these phenomena that they're discussing.

00:40:38.109 --> 00:40:43.190
And so the last thing we would want to do
as NLP practitioners would be to extract this

00:40:43.190 --> 00:40:46.950
information and just say, look, it says, "John
loves Mary."

00:40:46.950 --> 00:40:52.510
I'm going to just extract that fact as being
true, and ignore the other part of the sentence.

00:40:52.510 --> 00:40:57.680
So taking into account this uncertainty of
language is very interesting.

00:40:57.680 --> 00:41:03.160
The final type of reasoning that's needed,
I think, is illustrated well by that tweet

00:41:03.160 --> 00:41:08.420
example, where this person says that they
just ate a bowl of Xanax for breakfast.

00:41:08.420 --> 00:41:14.369
It's true it's a well-formed sentence-- which,
by the way, does not happen often on Twitter,

00:41:14.369 --> 00:41:16.859
so that's good.

00:41:16.859 --> 00:41:22.619
Even the medication is well written in here,
so there's no problem of identifying that

00:41:22.619 --> 00:41:24.469
Xanax means Xanax.

00:41:24.469 --> 00:41:29.339
The question is, did this person actually
eat a bowl of Xanax for breakfast?

00:41:29.339 --> 00:41:30.660
Probably not.

00:41:30.660 --> 00:41:32.199
And so is it sarcasm?

00:41:32.199 --> 00:41:33.199
Is it humor?

00:41:33.199 --> 00:41:37.799
Or did this person actually mean that they
didn't eat a whole bowl of it, but maybe they

00:41:37.799 --> 00:41:40.190
ate a lot of Xanax?

00:41:40.190 --> 00:41:47.430
And maybe we would want to pay attention to
that type of signal when doing monitoring

00:41:47.430 --> 00:41:49.440
of Twitter.

00:41:49.440 --> 00:41:53.460
We want to be able to build models that can
recognize that there's something uncertain

00:41:53.460 --> 00:41:57.249
about this information.

00:41:57.249 --> 00:42:05.440
So approaches to health NLP-- there are actually
many, many different approaches out there.

00:42:05.440 --> 00:42:12.660
And I made kind of like an editorial choice
in focusing on two types of methods.

00:42:12.660 --> 00:42:17.799
And I'll talk about a third one in the current
developments, but keep in mind, again, I'm

00:42:17.799 --> 00:42:19.369
just giving you an overview.

00:42:19.369 --> 00:42:24.400
And I definitely suggest that if you're interested
in the field of natural language processing

00:42:24.400 --> 00:42:30.099
in the health domain, there are wonderful
classes online and tutorials.

00:42:30.099 --> 00:42:37.180
And I'm happy to, if you want to get in touch
with me, to point you to some of them.

00:42:37.180 --> 00:42:45.589
So a task for which a lot of methods have
been designed over time in clinical texts

00:42:45.589 --> 00:42:49.099
and clinical NLP is one of information extraction.

00:42:49.099 --> 00:42:54.660
So the task is, if I have a sentence like
this one, "Patient should come back if severe

00:42:54.660 --> 00:43:02.029
facial rash occurs," I want to be able to
do some what's called "named entity recognition,"

00:43:02.029 --> 00:43:07.279
meaning that I recognize that "facial rash"
is a disorder.

00:43:07.279 --> 00:43:10.480
It's a type of problem that can happen to
the patient.

00:43:10.480 --> 00:43:14.730
Not only that, it's a particular concept in
a terminology.

00:43:14.730 --> 00:43:20.250
In this case, I'm showing you a concept in
the UMLS, the Unified Medical Language System,

00:43:20.250 --> 00:43:26.299
which is a really large terminology.

00:43:26.299 --> 00:43:31.729
And maybe I'd like to extract other modifiers
about these particular disorders.

00:43:31.729 --> 00:43:36.599
So in this case, maybe I would want to identify
that "severe" is an

00:43:36.599 --> 00:43:39.539
important modifier here.

00:43:39.539 --> 00:43:45.440
And the "if" here is also, as you can imagine,
quite important, and is indicating that this

00:43:45.440 --> 00:43:47.390
is a conditional type of disorder.

00:43:47.390 --> 00:43:53.619
In other words, the patient actually does
not have a facial rash, but the facial rash

00:43:53.619 --> 00:43:58.829
is discussed, nevertheless, in the context
of this particular patient.

00:43:58.829 --> 00:44:02.210
So what other type of modifiers have been
thought about?

00:44:02.210 --> 00:44:04.150
Many, many different ones.

00:44:04.150 --> 00:44:07.329
This is an example of a set of modifiers for
disorders.

00:44:07.329 --> 00:44:10.089
We would want it normalized to a terminology.

00:44:10.089 --> 00:44:17.279
We would want to be able to identify negation,
who the subject of that disorder is, who is

00:44:17.279 --> 00:44:21.079
the disorder attributed to is another way
to think about it.

00:44:21.079 --> 00:44:23.270
Is there uncertainty?

00:44:23.270 --> 00:44:30.380
Is there some sort of progression or course
mentioned about that disorder?

00:44:30.380 --> 00:44:32.180
What is the severity of the disorder?

00:44:32.180 --> 00:44:35.369
Is it conditional, like in the example earlier?

00:44:35.369 --> 00:44:40.289
Or is it actually not a disorder, like in
this example, when we say, "The patient goes

00:44:40.289 --> 00:44:41.719
to the HIV clinic."

00:44:41.719 --> 00:44:48.019
Really, it says "HIV," and HIV is a disorder,
but HIV clinic is really an identity recognition

00:44:48.019 --> 00:44:51.479
we would want to pay attention to.

00:44:51.479 --> 00:44:57.640
And if there is a particular body location
that's affected by the disorder, like facial

00:44:57.640 --> 00:45:03.999
rash, facial is actually part of the important
body location we would want to extract here.

00:45:03.999 --> 00:45:09.380
So there's been a lot of work in natural language
processing to kind of define this type of

00:45:09.380 --> 00:45:17.089
schemas to figure out what it is that we want
to extract in clinical texts like this one.

00:45:17.089 --> 00:45:18.630
That's one type of work.

00:45:18.630 --> 00:45:22.950
And typically, this is done through lots of
annotations of text.

00:45:22.950 --> 00:45:26.670
And there's a whole field of research about
how to do these annotations in a way that

00:45:26.670 --> 00:45:31.069
is correct, in ways that we can trust the
type of annotation, that we found that the

00:45:31.069 --> 00:45:36.709
schemas are generic enough, and can be applied
to many different types of information extraction

00:45:36.709 --> 00:45:37.709
systems.

00:45:37.709 --> 00:45:42.869
And then, of course, we want to be able to
build methods that are going to actually use

00:45:42.869 --> 00:45:49.699
the schema, and then go to a new type of text,
and identify those named entities being mentioned,

00:45:49.699 --> 00:45:51.640
along with all of these modifiers.

00:45:51.640 --> 00:45:58.260
This is a really, really large field of research
in clinical NLP.

00:45:58.260 --> 00:46:05.130
And so I just am giving here literally three
examples of famous papers here on this domain.

00:46:05.130 --> 00:46:07.300
But there are many, many more.

00:46:07.300 --> 00:46:13.849
The examples I'm giving here is from the takes
from the cTAKES system, which is actually

00:46:13.849 --> 00:46:14.849
open source.

00:46:14.849 --> 00:46:17.619
And you can try, if you have any clinical
texts.

00:46:17.619 --> 00:46:25.309
And the reason I like it is it kind of shows
you all of the different part of the pipeline

00:46:25.309 --> 00:46:29.339
that the system goes through in order to do
this named entity recognition.

00:46:29.339 --> 00:46:37.969
So there is a sentence here that says, "Family
history," FX is an abbreviation for family

00:46:37.969 --> 00:46:43.339
history, "of obesity, but no family history
of coronary artery disease."

00:46:43.339 --> 00:46:46.759
So the first question is, how do we talk nice?

00:46:46.759 --> 00:46:52.509
And this, I believe in the text framing lecture,
you guys had some discussion of that.

00:46:52.509 --> 00:46:58.529
We want to then identify the part of speech
of all of those different sentences, the different

00:46:58.529 --> 00:47:02.640
words in the
sentences, and maybe do what's called a shallow

00:47:02.640 --> 00:47:07.940
parsing, meaning we would identify not the
whole syntactic structure of the sentence,

00:47:07.940 --> 00:47:11.319
but rather what are the primary noun phrases.

00:47:11.319 --> 00:47:18.869
So a noun phrase would be a set of words that
together refer to a noun, a [INAUDIBLE] noun.

00:47:18.869 --> 00:47:24.249
And from there, we would then go and look
for particular noun phrases that can be mapped

00:47:24.249 --> 00:47:25.700
to a terminology, like here.

00:47:25.700 --> 00:47:30.500
So obesity is found, and coronary artery disease
is found.

00:47:30.500 --> 00:47:34.189
But also coronary artery, and artery, and
diseases.

00:47:34.189 --> 00:47:38.680
And all of those are potential named entities
we would want to identify.

00:47:38.680 --> 00:47:47.449
Furthermore, the status for obesity is that
it's a family history, as opposed to that

00:47:47.449 --> 00:47:54.729
patient has obesity, and it's not negated.

00:47:54.729 --> 00:48:01.380
So nowadays, I think that the state of the
art type of named entity recognition systems

00:48:01.380 --> 00:48:03.549
are using sequence labellers.

00:48:03.549 --> 00:48:11.619
And again, [INAUDIBLE] kind of mentioned it,
and so I thought I would move on from there.

00:48:11.619 --> 00:48:17.109
But you can see what I mean here by sequence
label, where I'm able to go through each of

00:48:17.109 --> 00:48:24.219
these terms, each of these words, and I'm
slowly building up these particular term,

00:48:24.219 --> 00:48:29.099
"coronary artery disease," by saying that
this is the beginning of my entity, and I'm

00:48:29.099 --> 00:48:36.119
going to keep in my sequence of terms until
I feel like it doesn't belong to the named

00:48:36.119 --> 00:48:37.119
entity anymore.

00:48:37.119 --> 00:48:42.270
You've heard me refer to terminologies quite
a lot.

00:48:42.270 --> 00:48:48.769
And this is outside the scope of this lecture,
but you realize, I hope, that we are relying

00:48:48.769 --> 00:48:54.640
heavily in this type of clinical task on lexicons,
or terminologies, or ontologies.

00:48:54.640 --> 00:49:01.459
And one huge challenge is, how do we ensure
that the lexicon actually captures the language

00:49:01.459 --> 00:49:04.440
of the corpus under study?

00:49:04.440 --> 00:49:11.960
The UMLS is very large, but it's also quite
specific, for example, to clinical and technical

00:49:11.960 --> 00:49:12.960
type of language.

00:49:12.960 --> 00:49:17.799
And so now, when we are dealing working in
online health communities or on Twitter, we

00:49:17.799 --> 00:49:22.689
need to figure out ways to either augment
or shelter the UMLS.

00:49:22.689 --> 00:49:26.059
And NLP can help for this as well.

00:49:26.059 --> 00:49:31.269
Another type of approach that's been really
helpful and I've mentioned is one of probabilistic

00:49:31.269 --> 00:49:32.430
topic model.

00:49:32.430 --> 00:49:36.779
And I would like to refer you for this of
all the work of David Blei, and all of the

00:49:36.779 --> 00:49:39.039
people who worked on this for the past 10
plus years.

00:49:39.039 --> 00:49:45.839
The idea here is a very different approach
to clinical named entity recognition, and

00:49:45.839 --> 00:49:53.069
is looking at in ways completely unsupervised,
and is trying to find patterns of text, and

00:49:53.069 --> 00:50:01.180
in particular, is trying to identify clusters
of words that tend to co-occur quite often

00:50:01.180 --> 00:50:08.029
with each other, such that the documents that
they appear on are considered to be a probabilistic

00:50:08.029 --> 00:50:12.140
mixture of those clusters or topics.

00:50:12.140 --> 00:50:18.430
And so you can add the output of your topic
modeling, or in this case, latent Dirichlet

00:50:18.430 --> 00:50:24.650
allocation, you can identify that all of these
different words together are very likely to

00:50:24.650 --> 00:50:26.749
be part of that topic.

00:50:26.749 --> 00:50:34.269
And moreover, we could think of that topic,
as humans, as being about genetics, or evolution,

00:50:34.269 --> 00:50:37.369
or disease in particular for that example.

00:50:37.369 --> 00:50:40.599
I have not talked at all about evaluation.

00:50:40.599 --> 00:50:46.499
But this is something that people think a
lot about in health natural language processing.

00:50:46.499 --> 00:50:48.400
There's a lot of task-based evaluation.

00:50:48.400 --> 00:50:52.529
So when we do progression of disease, we think
of

00:50:52.529 --> 00:50:57.960
task-based evaluation as did I predict who
is going to progress accurately?

00:50:57.960 --> 00:50:58.960
And that's fine.

00:50:58.960 --> 00:51:05.099
But when we think about the core NLP methods,
we do usually need gold-standard annotations

00:51:05.099 --> 00:51:10.299
to do some sort of validation, or internal
validation, or intrinsic evaluation of the

00:51:10.299 --> 00:51:12.009
core NLP methods.

00:51:12.009 --> 00:51:13.349
And again, it's the whole topic.

00:51:13.349 --> 00:51:16.690
So I'm not touching on it, but I'm mentioning
it here.

00:51:16.690 --> 00:51:22.739
So I want to finish with the current developments
in health NLP and NLP research.

00:51:22.739 --> 00:51:32.089
And this is a very exciting type of research
that is happening for the past less than five

00:51:32.089 --> 00:51:41.930
years, actually, and which is the event of
neural nets in the world of linguistics.

00:51:41.930 --> 00:51:49.849
And so I felt like I couldn't really point
you to very specific papers.

00:51:49.849 --> 00:51:54.939
But if you are interested in the context of
deep learning and natural language processing,

00:51:54.939 --> 00:51:58.939
Richard Socher at Stanford has an excellent
class.

00:51:58.939 --> 00:52:00.940
And all material is given online.

00:52:00.940 --> 00:52:07.369
So I suggest that you go and either read,
or even try to do the homework.

00:52:07.369 --> 00:52:13.160
But I thought that basically, there's four
areas that are very interesting, I think,

00:52:13.160 --> 00:52:17.369
from an NLP standpoint as far as new areas
of work.

00:52:17.369 --> 00:52:20.359
One is a better or improved language model.

00:52:20.359 --> 00:52:26.730
We know already that we need a good contextual
representation of what words are.

00:52:26.730 --> 00:52:31.279
And now, through neural nets, we have better
contextual representations, not only of the

00:52:31.279 --> 00:52:36.609
words, but what the sequence of the words,
or even sequences of characters, represent.

00:52:36.609 --> 00:52:39.510
And that has been shown to be extremely powerful.

00:52:39.510 --> 00:52:42.269
We have better sequenced models.

00:52:42.269 --> 00:52:46.959
And so remember that named entity recognition
task, where we're looking for a sequence of

00:52:46.959 --> 00:52:52.739
words that together define a medical term,
for instance, recurrent neural nets, either

00:52:52.739 --> 00:52:57.959
on words or on characters, are very exciting,
because it can capture rich, long-distance

00:52:57.959 --> 00:53:06.329
dependencies in text, which was not possible
with different models, like CRF, for example.

00:53:06.329 --> 00:53:12.039
Finally, there is really interesting work
in learning models for mixed modalities.

00:53:12.039 --> 00:53:15.390
So what do I mean by mixed modality?

00:53:15.390 --> 00:53:19.989
Things like text plus images, text plus laboratory
tests, et cetera.

00:53:19.989 --> 00:53:27.359
And so we've had some example in the applications,
where we know that by incorporating all this

00:53:27.359 --> 00:53:36.400
metadata, if you want, about a patient, or
about an individual online, we can actually

00:53:36.400 --> 00:53:41.740
predict better or understand better what a
person is talking about, or what a doctor

00:53:41.740 --> 00:53:43.809
is talking about with respect to a patient.

00:53:43.809 --> 00:53:49.650
And finally, there is interesting new type
of machine learning.

00:53:49.650 --> 00:53:52.980
I'm thinking about a adversarial neural nets
and reinforcement learning, in particular,

00:53:52.980 --> 00:54:02.689
that are starting to show some very useful
progress in NLP.

00:54:02.689 --> 00:54:05.380
So again, I'm not pointing you to any specific
papers.

00:54:05.380 --> 00:54:11.749
But if you're interested, there are some classes
online for you to look at.

00:54:11.749 --> 00:54:17.670
So I want to conclude by telling you that
language has pervasive.

00:54:17.670 --> 00:54:19.969
It's hard to process automatically.

00:54:19.969 --> 00:54:21.660
And sometimes, it's also hard for humans.

00:54:21.660 --> 00:54:24.180
But that's because there is ambiguity.

00:54:24.180 --> 00:54:26.420
There is variability and vagueness.

00:54:26.420 --> 00:54:30.410
But there is much progress that has been done.

00:54:30.410 --> 00:54:34.119
And I'll finish by saying that this is an
active field of research, both

00:54:34.119 --> 00:54:39.439
in terms of the core NLP methods that are
being developed, but also in ways of thinking

00:54:39.439 --> 00:54:45.459
of how to leverage texts in general for biomedical
knowledge discovery, characterizing diseases

00:54:45.459 --> 00:54:48.759
and behavior, improving care, et cetera.

00:54:48.759 --> 00:54:50.200
So with that, I'll stop.

00:54:50.200 --> 00:54:52.469
And hopefully, there is enough time for questions.

00:54:52.469 --> 00:55:00.029
Thank you, Dr. Elhadad for a great talk and
overview on NLP and the medical and health

00:55:00.029 --> 00:55:01.029
domains.

00:55:01.029 --> 00:55:04.299
So we have one question for you from the audience.

00:55:04.299 --> 00:55:07.400
This first question is regarding privacy issues.

00:55:07.400 --> 00:55:13.119
And so a lot of the examples that you gave
are from social media like reddit and other

00:55:13.119 --> 00:55:16.769
areas where you might be monitoring forums
and such.

00:55:16.769 --> 00:55:24.039
Can you address how issues related to privacy
and potentially de-identification are addressed

00:55:24.039 --> 00:55:25.359
in this type of work?

00:55:25.359 --> 00:55:29.729
Obviously, that has applications also, more
specifically when you're monitoring clinical

00:55:29.729 --> 00:55:30.729
records.

00:55:30.729 --> 00:55:34.670
But if you could touch broadly on that, that
would be appreciated.

00:55:34.670 --> 00:55:38.789
Great, yes, that's a great question.

00:55:38.789 --> 00:55:40.839
And you're right, I didn't talk about this.

00:55:40.839 --> 00:55:43.969
I gave you an overview of different papers
out there.

00:55:43.969 --> 00:55:52.720
And everyone has their own [INAUDIBLE] of
their own institution that usually makes sure

00:55:52.720 --> 00:55:58.299
that the methods are being respectful of the
participants, in this case, the people who

00:55:58.299 --> 00:56:02.119
write those texts, or the people about which
these texts are written.

00:56:02.119 --> 00:56:08.289
So when it comes to online communities and
social media, most of the papers I've seen

00:56:08.289 --> 00:56:15.619
deal with publicly available data sets and
text corpora.

00:56:15.619 --> 00:56:22.319
So for example, this breast cancer data set
that I mentioned very early on is completely

00:56:22.319 --> 00:56:23.799
available online.

00:56:23.799 --> 00:56:29.269
Twitter is available if you don't pay attention
to private messages, et cetera.

00:56:29.269 --> 00:56:37.279
There are some questions about how would you--
say you go and crawl in the entirety of an

00:56:37.279 --> 00:56:39.049
online health community.

00:56:39.049 --> 00:56:41.279
How do you go about distributing these data?

00:56:41.279 --> 00:56:44.079
As researchers, we do not have the right to
distribute it.

00:56:44.079 --> 00:56:50.979
We need to have, somehow, the agreement of
the people who created and are monitoring

00:56:50.979 --> 00:56:53.160
these data.

00:56:53.160 --> 00:56:57.640
And so people have been looking a lot into
how to de-indentify texts.

00:56:57.640 --> 00:57:03.400
And there are some, both commercial and research,
identifiers out there.

00:57:03.400 --> 00:57:08.329
And it turns out it's a very institution-specific
type of thing, where some institutions are

00:57:08.329 --> 00:57:16.069
OK with letting you distribute your data if
you de-identified it, and others are not.

00:57:16.069 --> 00:57:19.140
There are de-identified clinical corpora out
there.

00:57:19.140 --> 00:57:23.170
[? Minick ?] is one of them.

00:57:23.170 --> 00:57:28.450
And there are publicly available online health
communities out there.

00:57:28.450 --> 00:57:33.039
Great, so our second question is actually
about evaluation.

00:57:33.039 --> 00:57:36.229
What is the typical error rate in interpreting
these texts?

00:57:36.229 --> 00:57:40.890
As you apply actually language processing
techniques, obviously, it's not necessarily

00:57:40.890 --> 00:57:41.890
perfect.

00:57:41.890 --> 00:57:46.059
And as you pointed out, there is a lot of
inherent vagueness to the texts.

00:57:46.059 --> 00:57:48.819
So even humans are not necessarily perfect.

00:57:48.819 --> 00:57:52.599
But can you discuss what the typical error
rate might be?

00:57:52.599 --> 00:57:57.890
And are there any examples where any misinterpretations
have actually led to bad decisions?

00:57:57.890 --> 00:58:05.910
OK, so I feel like there are kind of two questions--
unintended consequences of recognition of

00:58:05.910 --> 00:58:09.019
something and what is the typical error rate.

00:58:09.019 --> 00:58:13.179
It's hard to say what typical is.

00:58:13.179 --> 00:58:18.499
If you think of a named entity recognition
system, it's typically built with a terminology

00:58:18.499 --> 00:58:21.009
in mind for a particular type of text.

00:58:21.009 --> 00:58:28.029
And so if I type UMLS and cTAKES and apply
it to-- or MetaMap or MedLEE-- and apply it

00:58:28.029 --> 00:58:36.420
to an autism community, I might find that
I have an error rate of, say, 40% in F-measure.

00:58:36.420 --> 00:58:41.380
But if I were to do exactly the same, and
apply it to clinical notes, I would have an

00:58:41.380 --> 00:58:45.289
error rate of 85 F-measure.

00:58:45.289 --> 00:58:57.329
So it's difficult to say, without thinking
of the task, the corpus, and the terminologies,

00:58:57.329 --> 00:59:02.270
for example, how accurate typically an named
entity recognition system is.

00:59:02.270 --> 00:59:07.109
And what this is pointing at is that there's
not a whole lot of benchmarking in health

00:59:07.109 --> 00:59:08.109
NLP.

00:59:08.109 --> 00:59:10.730
That's something that the community is working
on heavily.

00:59:10.730 --> 00:59:19.999
So there have been, for the past few years,
data sets that have been deidentified in the

00:59:19.999 --> 00:59:29.240
case of clinical texts where gold-standard
annotations are provided for people to share.

00:59:29.240 --> 00:59:35.929
And there are challenges that many different
research groups try different methods.

00:59:35.929 --> 00:59:43.230
But everyone agrees to use the same evaluation
metrics on the same benchmark annotations

00:59:43.230 --> 00:59:44.230
and texts.

00:59:44.230 --> 00:59:47.839
And we can then compare and understand which
methods work better than another.

00:59:47.839 --> 00:59:56.459
I would say, though, that we're probably not
as far ahead as we could be compared to other

00:59:56.459 --> 01:00:02.309
domains of NLP and other areas of machine
learning, in part because of those privacy

01:00:02.309 --> 01:00:04.170
issues, for sure.

01:00:04.170 --> 01:00:07.559
Great, so we're actually at 10 o'clock now.

01:00:07.559 --> 01:00:12.430
So being mindful of the time, thank you again
so much for giving this talk.

01:00:12.430 --> 01:00:15.099
And we'll close it here.

01:00:15.099 --> 01:00:16.779
Happy holidays, everyone.

01:00:16.779 --> 01:00:21.729
We will be back in January, 2017, with the
next set of talks.

01:00:21.729 --> 01:00:28.130
Thank you, and bye, everyone.

